00:54:51.600 --> 00:54:56.666
A mask is essentially a mapping of all
of the pixels that exist in the Sprite.

00:54:57.133 --> 00:54:58.700
So whenever we draw
something on the screen,

00:54:58.700 --> 00:55:00.500
we're really drawing a rectangle, right?

00:55:00.500 --> 00:55:05.933
But the rectangle may not have
nontransparent pixels, right?

00:55:05.933 --> 00:55:08.300
So only part of the rectangle
is actually filled in.

00:55:08.300 --> 00:55:12.066
Hence why we get kind of a circular image,
a dynamic image, whatever.

00:55:12.066 --> 00:55:15.500
So what this mask tells us
is where there's actually images

00:55:15.500 --> 00:55:17.766
or where is not
where there's actually pixels. Sorry.

00:55:17.766 --> 00:55:20.800
And this mask allows us to perform
pixel perfect collision

00:55:20.800 --> 00:55:25.300
because we can overlap it with another
mask and make sure that we only say two

00:55:25.300 --> 00:55:30.833
objects collide if pixels are colliding,
not if the rectangular box is colliding.

00:55:31.300 --> 00:55:33.933
If we did rectangular collision,
then it constantly looks like

00:55:33.933 --> 00:55:36.100
we're hitting something
even when we're not.

00:55:36.100 --> 00:55:37.166
Because the rectangle for

00:55:37.166 --> 00:55:41.300
our character is larger than where
all of the pixels for our character are.

00:55:41.600 --> 00:55:43.533
You've probably seen this
in a lot of games before,

00:55:43.533 --> 00:55:45.766
but what the mask does is solve
that problem for us

00:55:45.966 --> 00:55:48.533
and allow us to do this
kind of pixel perfect collision.

00:55:48.833 --> 00:55:51.400
It's very important,
